  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Mechanistic chain from CFTR loss‑of‑function to inflammation in cystic fibrosis**\n\n* **CFTR dysfunction → altered epithelial ion transport, dehydrated mucus and obstructive pathology** – Mouse models with targeted CFTR deletion show mucus accumulation, disturbed motility and small‑bowel bacterial overgrowth, all downstream of the channel defect【PMID:24548777】 (animal). Reviews of human and murine disease note that the same “obstruction‑infection‑inflammation” triad seen in airways also drives intestinal disease【PMID:23788646】【PMID:23142604】 (human/animal synthesis).\n\n* **Epithelial (and immune‑cell) CFTR loss → dysbiosis of the gut microbiota** – In cftr‑knockout mice, 16S rRNA sequencing revealed that loss of CFTR in epithelial cells reshapes the large‑intestinal microbiota, while loss in immune cells drives dysbiosis in the small intestine【PMID:36289287】 (animal). The same mouse studies also report bacterial overgrowth as a consequence of mucus stasis【PMID:24548777】.\n\n* **Dysbiosis and bacterial overgrowth → activation of innate immune pathways and local inflammation** – The intestinal inflammation observed in CF mice is described as “altered innate immune responses” that accompany dysbiosis and overgrowth【PMID:24548777】 (animal). Human studies of people with CF, even without GI symptoms, demonstrate chronic low‑grade intestinal inflammation that co‑occurs with an abnormal microbiota【PMID:35160099】 (human).\n\n* **Local gut inflammation → systemic and extra‑intestinal inflammatory sequelae** – The authors of the gut‑inflammation review propose that chronic intestinal inflammation can act as a driver for systemic inflammation in CF【PMID:24548777】 (inference from animal/human data). The gut‑lung axis literature further links intestinal microbial and inflammatory changes to lung immune dysregulation in CF patients【PMID:34339302】 (human).\n\n* **Overall inference: CFTR loss → epithelial/mucosal dysfunction → dysbiosis → innate immune activation → local and systemic inflammation** – The convergent evidence from murine genetic models, human observational studies, and mechanistic reviews collectively map each step, supporting a causal cascade rather than a mere association.\n\n**Conclusion:** Multiple animal and human studies delineate a stepwise pathway from CFTR deficiency to intestinal dysbiosis and innate immune activation, establishing inflammation as a mechanistic consequence of CFTR loss in cystic fibrosis."
  },
